exports[`every form has an instruction > charges-reply 1`] = `
"Form: Reply to a memorandum of charges / आरोप ज्ञापन का उत्तर (id: charges-reply).
Used by: An officer, on their own behalf.
CSMOP 2022, sixteenth edition, paragraph 8.4(1).
CSMOP prescribes no format for this document. This form follows the format of the nearest prescribed one; say nothing that claims the Manual specifies it.

Structure:
1. the place and date
2. the addressee block
3. the subject line, one sentence, ending in “regarding.” or “के संबंध में।”
4. the salutation
5. the body paragraphs
6. the subscription
7. the signature block — name, designation, telephone and e-mail
8. the list of enclosures

Rules:
- Write in the FIRST person — this form is one named officer writing to another.
- This form carries NO salutation.
- This form carries NO subscription.
- This form carries NO urgency grading.
- Hindi register: use the administrative Hindi of the Central Secretariat (Rajbhasha) — "कार्यालय ज्ञापन", "अवर सचिव", "एतद्द्वारा" — and not everyday or literary Hindi. Keep file numbers, section numbers and English proper names in their original form.
- Leave anything you were not told as four underscores (____). Never invent a file number, a date, an amount, a name or a designation.

The document will be marked against this checklist, by the app, after you answer. Every item below must pass:
- Each article of charge is answered separately — An article of charge that is not specifically denied is treated as admitted. Answering the memorandum as a whole is the commonest and costliest defect in a defence statement.
- Whether an oral inquiry is desired is stated — The disciplinary authority has to record this; leaving it out means the decision is taken without knowing what the charged officer wanted.
- The subject is stated — The specimens in Appendix 8.1 carry a subject line above the body on every form that is addressed to anyone.
- Paragraphs are serially numbered — The specimens leave the first paragraph unnumbered and number the rest from 2.
- No placeholder is left unfilled — A draft that goes out with {{…}} still in it has not been read.

What the officer has already written. Complete it — do not replace what is there:
- place: New Delhi
- date: 2026-08-28
- addressee: The Disciplinary Authority
Department of Personnel and Training
North Block, New Delhi
- subject: Written statement of defence in respect of the memorandum of charges dated 12.08.2026.
- paras: This written statement of defence is submitted in reply to the memorandum of charges No. A-11012/7/2026-Vig. dated 12.08.2026, served on me on 18.08.2026, under rule 16 of the Central Civil Services (Classification, Control and Appeal) Rules, 1965.
# Reply to each article of charge
For the purpose of my defence I request that the following documents may be made available to me: the file movement register of the Section for the period 01.07.2026 to 31.07.2026
I desire an oral inquiry in the matter. The statement is submitted with respect, and I pray that the charges may be dropped.
- signatoryName: A.B.C.
- signatoryDesignation: Section Officer
- phone: 011-2309 2590
- email: abc@nic.in
- enclosures: A copy of the memorandum of charges dated 12.08.2026.

Still empty: memoNumber, memoDate, servedDate, ccaRule, articleReplies, inquiryWanted."
`;

exports[`every form has an instruction > circular 1`] = `
"Form: Circular / परिपत्र (id: circular).
Used by: A Department bringing one instruction to the notice of everyone at once — all sections, all officers, or all Ministries.
CSMOP 2022, sixteenth edition, paragraphs 8.4(3), Appendix 8.1.
CSMOP prescribes no format for this document. This form follows the format of the nearest prescribed one; say nothing that claims the Manual specifies it.

Structure:
1. the urgency grading, at the very top
2. the file number
3. the letterhead — Ministry, Department and office
4. the place and date
5. the form’s own title, centred
6. the subject line, one sentence, ending in “regarding.” or “के संबंध में।”
7. the body paragraphs
8. the signature block — name, designation, telephone and e-mail
9. the addressee block
10. the list of enclosures
11. the copy-to list

Rules:
- Write in the THIRD person throughout — "the undersigned", "it is requested", never "I" or "we".
- This form carries NO salutation.
- This form carries NO subscription.
- An urgency grading (Immediate / Priority / Top Priority — तत्काल / प्राथमिकता / परम अग्रता) may be shown at the top. Grade it only if the officer asked for one; an ungraded communication is the normal case.
- Hindi register: use the administrative Hindi of the Central Secretariat (Rajbhasha) — "कार्यालय ज्ञापन", "अवर सचिव", "एतद्द्वारा" — and not everyday or literary Hindi. Keep file numbers, section numbers and English proper names in their original form.
- Leave anything you were not told as four underscores (____). Never invent a file number, a date, an amount, a name or a designation.
- A circular says the same thing to many addressees at once, so it names no individual and asks for nothing that would differ between them.

The document will be marked against this checklist, by the app, after you answer. Every item below must pass:
- Written in the third person — An Office Memorandum is written in the third person and bears no salutation or subscription.
- Number and date are on the draft — Every draft put up on a file bears the file number, and the number and date of the last communication in the series must be quoted.
- The subject is stated — The specimens in Appendix 8.1 carry a subject line above the body on every form that is addressed to anyone.
- Paragraphs are serially numbered — The specimens leave the first paragraph unnumbered and number the rest from 2.
- Addressed to everyone it is meant for — A circular that names no addressee is an O.M. that will be filed and forgotten.
- Signature block carries designation, telephone and email — The name, designation, telephone number, fax number and e-mail address of the signing officer must invariably be on the draft.
- Enclosures mentioned in the text are listed — A draft should clearly specify the enclosures which are to accompany the fair copy, and the number goes at the bottom left.
- No placeholder is left unfilled — A draft that goes out with {{…}} still in it has not been read.

What the officer has already written. Complete it — do not replace what is there:
- urgency: none
- fileNumber: A-11011/2/2026-Estt.
- ministry: Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions
- department: Department of Personnel and Training
- place: New Delhi
- date: 2026-08-28
- subject: Observance of office timings and use of the biometric attendance system — regarding.
- paras: It has been observed that office timings are not being adhered to uniformly across sections. All officers and staff are reminded that the prescribed office hours are 9.00 a.m. to 5.30 p.m., with a half-hour lunch break.
Attendance is to be marked on the biometric attendance system on arrival and before leaving. Late arrival on more than two occasions in a month will be regulated in accordance with the instructions on the subject.
All Heads of Divisions are requested to bring this to the notice of every officer and member of the staff working under them by 15.09.2026.
- signatoryName: A.B.C.
- signatoryDesignation: Under Secretary to the Govt. of India
- phone: 011-2309 2590
- email: us-estt@nic.in
- addressee: All officers and staff of the Department
All Heads of Divisions
- copyTo: Notice board
Departmental website
Guard file

